        exp: "Rule: A decisive split of authority governs felony murder when a non-felon (such as a police officer, security guard, or crime victim) fires the fatal shot. Under the majority Agency Theory, felony murder applies ONLY IF the fatal act is committed by one of the felons or an agent acting in furtherance of the common criminal conspiracy; if a guard shoots a co-felon, the surviving felon is not guilty of felony murder. HOWEVER, this jurisdiction explicitly follows the minority PROXIMATE CAUSE THEORY. Under the proximate cause theory, a surviving felon is criminally liable for ANY death that proximately and foreseeably results from the commission of, or flight from, an inherently dangerous felony, even if the fatal shot is fired by a police officer, guard, or victim in self-defense. Because the armed confrontation and fatal shot were a foreseeable consequence of the nighttime commercial burglary, Defendant is guilty of felony murder under the proximate cause theory (Option B). Option A is incorrect because this jurisdiction rejects the agency theory. Option C is incorrect because the Redline rule applies in agency jurisdictions, not in proximate cause jurisdictions. Option D is incorrect because the conviction under the proximate cause rule is felony murder, not manslaughter."
