        fp: "A homeowner contracted in writing with an industrial tree-clearing service to fell, limb, and remove three mature, 90-foot dead ponderosa pine trees standing near his driveway for $6,000. Under the contract, work was to begin on May 10 and be completed by May 15. On May 12, after cutting down the first tree, the tree service's commercial diesel log crane suffered a fractured hydraulic boom arm. The tree service foreman approached the homeowner and stated: 'Our main crane is out of commission. Due to current high rental rates, I cannot afford to bring in a replacement crane unless you agree in writing to pay an extra $1,500.' Desiring to have the dead trees removed before an upcoming garden party, the homeowner signed a written addendum promising to pay a total of $7,500 upon completion. The tree service rented a replacement crane and completed the removal of all three trees on May 15 in full compliance with the contract. The homeowner paid the original $6,000, but refused to pay the additional $1,500, asserting the common law pre-existing duty rule.",
